- **AlphaGo beat added** at the top of "The tanks on the lawn" — Seoul 2016, Move 37, framed as the peak the war interrupted, with a Liechtenstein callback so it pays off the games-as-laboratory thesis rather than resume-dumping.
- **Weapons-pledge clause cashed out, and it's sharper than the reviewer guessed:** verification showed Google didn't just drop the pledge in February 2025 — the blog post announcing the reversal was co-signed by Hassabis himself ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/02/04/google-removes-pledge-to-not-use-ai-for-weapons-surveillance.html), [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2025/02/04/business/google-ai-weapons-surveillance)). The draft now says so: "The man who made a weapons ban the price of his company co-signed its repeal."
- **"Church hall" was unattested** in the research file, so all three callbacks now anchor to Liechtenstein instead. Same for "unpaid" PhD — dropped in both spots rather than risk an error.
- Sam Altman is now named and linked in the ChatGPT sentence (verified he's published via people-wall.json), and a one-line Thinking Game mention landed in the Nobel section with no viewership claim.
- Trims: cut the third thesis restatement ("Four times, the same trade...") and compressed the Nobel section's Liechtenstein re-narration to one sentence.

**Still open before publish:** the Mallaby book quotes (David Silver, "wartime") are still sourced via the LessWrong review's transcription rather than the book itself — that needs the fact-check pass the working notes already flagged. The two lint warns are pre-existing and benign (both comparatives are "looked like" instances; 2 of the 7 internal links are template-mandated).
